<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelberht of Kent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 616</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Ethelbert of Kent, Saint Æthelberht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelberht was king of Kent from about 589 to 616 and the first English king to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Married to the Frankish Christian princess Bertha, he welcomed Augustine's mission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> in 597 and gave it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> as its base. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> names him the third overlord of the southern English, and his law code is the earliest surviving document written in English.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent.jpg" alt="Æthelberht of Kent" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giogo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Ethelbert_jub%C3%A9_Rochester_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
